e literal besides `0`) = 1\n\nTotal: 7+12+4+5+6+1 = **35**\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```typescript\nconst requireInsuredItem = (policy: Policy, itemType: string): PolicyItem => {\n  const item = findPolicyItem(policy, itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`Damaged item not part of policy: ${itemType}`);\n  }\n  return item;\n};\n\nexport const claim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  const rawPayout = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) =>\n      sum +\n      payoutForDamage(damage, requireInsuredItem(policy, damage.itemType)),\n    0\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(rawPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return {\n    payout: Math.floor(payout),\n    remainingCap: Math.floor(policy.remainingCap),\n  };\n};\n```\n- Bindings: policy, itemType, item, incident, sum, damage, payout = 7\n- Invocations: findPolicyItem, requireInsuredItem, payoutForDamage, reduce, Math.min, Math.floor(x2) = 7 → 14\n- Conditionals: 1 = 4\n- Loops: 1 = 5\n- Assignments: 1 = 6\n- Constants: 1 = 1\n\nTotal: 7+14+4+5+6+1 = **37**\n\nMass Change: 35 → 37 (delta +2)\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Naming: extracted `requireInsuredItem` to name the \"find-or-throw\" precondition explicitly — the `claim` reducer body now reads as a single intent-revealing expression (`sum + payoutForDamage(damage, requireInsuredItem(...))`) instead of a multi-statement block with an inline guard clause.\n- Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) took priority over the small APP mass increase (+2, from one extra invocation): the extraction makes `claim`'s reducer a pure expression, separates \"get a valid item\" from \"accumulate payout,\" and gives the validation logic a reusable, self-explanatory name.\n- Rule 3 (DRY): no duplication found elsewhere in the file worth extracting.\n- Rule 4 (Fewest Elements): the extraction adds one small function but removes a nested conditional block from the reducer, net readability win.\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refa